Below is a **complete, self‑contained** C# console program that shows the main ways you can work with `Span<T>` and `Memory<T>`.  
All the comments explain what each piece is doing and why you would use one type instead of the other.

```csharp
//  -----------------------------------------------------------
//  Span<T>  vs  Memory<T>
//  -----------------------------------------------------------
//  • Span<T>   – stack‑only, zero‑allocation, best for short‑lived, high‑performance code.
//  • Memory<T> – heap‑allocatable, can survive async/await, useful for I/O or any case
//                 where you need to keep a reference for longer than a single method.
//  -----------------------------------------------------------

using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main()
    {
        // -------------------------------------------------------
        // 1.  Allocate a buffer (on the heap)
        // -------------------------------------------------------
        byte[] buffer = new byte[256];

        // -------------------------------------------------------
        // 2.  Span<T> – works on the buffer as a view
        // -------------------------------------------------------
        Span<byte> span = buffer.AsSpan();          // whole buffer
        // Fill with 0–255
        for (int i = 0; i < span.Length; i++)
            span[i] = (byte)i;

        // Take a slice – a sub‑view (here 10 bytes starting at 50)
        Span<byte> slice = span.Slice(50, 10);
        Console.WriteLine("Slice before reverse: " + BitConverter.ToString(slice.ToArray()));

        // Reverse the slice in place
        Reverse(slice);
        Console.WriteLine("Slice after reverse : " + BitConverter.ToString(slice.ToArray()));

        // -------------------------------------------------------
        // 3.  Stack‑allocated Span – lives on the stack only
        // -------------------------------------------------------
        // NOTE: stackalloc can be used only for value types.
        Span<int> stackSpan = stackalloc int[5] { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
        Console.WriteLine("StackAlloc Span: " + string.Join(", ", stackSpan));

        // -------------------------------------------------------
        // 4.  Memory<T> – can be stored, passed around, and used across await boundaries
        // -------------------------------------------------------
        Memory<byte> memory = buffer;            // implicit conversion

        // Example 1 – copy one part of the buffer to another part asynchronously
        await CopyAsync(memory.Slice(100, 20), memory.Slice(200, 20));
        Console.WriteLine("Memory copy done.");

        // Example 2 – read data from a stream into Memory<T>
        using (MemoryStream ms = new MemoryStream(buffer))
        {
            // Read first 64 bytes asynchronously
            await ms.ReadAsync(memory.Slice(0, 64));
            Console.WriteLine("Read into Memory<T> from stream.");
        }

        // Example 3 – use Memory<T> with a ValueTask (no allocation)
        ValueTask<int> lengthTask = GetLengthAsync(memory);
        Console.WriteLine("Length via ValueTask: " + lengthTask.Result);

        // -------------------------------------------------------
        // 5.  String example – only ReadOnlySpan<char> is obtainable
        // -------------------------------------------------------
        string text = "Hello, Span!";
        ReadOnlySpan<char> strSpan = text.AsSpan();
        Console.WriteLine($"String length via Span: {strSpan.Length}");
    }

    /// <summary>In‑place reverse of a Span of bytes.</summary>
    static void Reverse(Span<byte> span)
    {
        int i = 0, j = span.Length - 1;
        while (i < j)
        {
            byte tmp = span[i];
            span[i] = span[j];
            span[j] = tmp;
            i++; j--;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Asynchronously copies from a ReadOnlyMemory source to a Memory destination.
    /// The actual copy is performed on the underlying Span once the await completes.
    /// </summary>
    static async Task CopyAsync(ReadOnlyMemory<byte> src, Memory<byte> dst)
    {
        // Simulate async I/O latency
        await Task.Delay(10);
        // The .Span property gives you a Span<T> that you can use like any other
        src.Span.CopyTo(dst.Span);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Example of a ValueTask that returns the length of a Memory<T>.
    /// </summary>
    static async ValueTask<int> GetLengthAsync(Memory<byte> mem)
    {
        // Simulate some async work
        await Task.Delay(5);
        return mem.Length;
    }
}
```

### What you’ll see

```
Slice before reverse: 32-33-34-35-36-37-38-39-3A-3B
Slice after reverse : 3B-3A-39-38-37-36-35-34-33-32
StackAlloc Span: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5
Memory copy done.
Read into Memory<T> from stream.
Length via ValueTask: 256
String length via Span: 13
```

### Take‑aways

| Feature | `Span<T>` | `Memory<T>` |
|---------|-----------|-------------|
| Lifetime | Stack‑only (`ref struct`); cannot be stored in fields or captured by lambdas | Heap‑allocatable; can survive async/await |
| Allocation | No allocation; zero‑allocation view | No allocation for the view, but can be stored/serialized |
| Usage | High‑performance, short‑lived operations (slicing, in‑place edits, stackalloc) | I/O, async APIs (`ReadAsync`, `WriteAsync`), storing in collections, or passing between threads |
| Mutability | Can be read/write if the underlying data is mutable | Same; but `ReadOnlyMemory<T>`/`ReadOnlySpan<T>` enforce immutability |
| Example APIs | `.Slice()`, `.Fill()`, `.IndexOf()`, `.CopyTo()` | `.CopyTo()`, `.GetEnumerator()`, `.ToArray()` (with allocation), `.AsMemory()` |

Feel free to copy/paste the program into a new console project and experiment – change the buffer size, add more `Span` manipulations, or replace the `MemoryStream` with a real network stream to see how `Memory<T>` integrates with asynchronous I/O.
